From da24ab919a5e5659b61e454bc3a95adf59fe9ba7 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Satish Babariya <themadengineer99@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 26 Aug 2026 10:11:32 +0530
Subject: [PATCH 3/5] Inspect SNI so policy is exact on shared addresses

The resolution ledger says which names an address was handed out for. That is
enough to refuse an address we never vouched for, but not enough to be precise
on a shared one: a CDN address may serve both an allowed name and an unrelated
one, and the dial check cannot tell which a connection wants.

The ClientHello can, and it is sent in the clear, so this needs no
interception, no certificate, and no key. The bytes are peeked and replayed
verbatim; nothing is decrypted.

pkg/sni parses the record with every length checked against the buffer, since
it is reading bytes the sandbox chose. Tests build real ClientHellos with the
standard library rather than fixtures, and cover truncation and malformed
records for panics.

SNI now classifies port 443 before the broker, since knowing the exact name is
what decides whether a credential is bound to it. A prefixConn replays the
peeked handshake into the broker's TLS server so its handshake sees the stream
from the start.
